            <sourceDesc>
                <p>Born digital. Shared authority file: the classification categories referred to
                    by the corpus files.</p>
            </sourceDesc>
        </fileDesc>
        <encodingDesc>
            <projectDesc>
                <p>This file holds the taxonomies shared by all the inscription files of the
                    corpus. An inscription points to a category by its identifier, in the form
                    <code>ana="../vocabularies/taxonomy.xml#centenarius"</code>. Category
                    identifiers are in Latin wherever the scholarly tradition supplies a received
                    term; translations and definitions are carried by the children of
                    <gi>catDesc</gi> and may be in any number of languages.</p>
                <p>Classes are declared here, individual entities are not. A particular
                    organization, person or place is recorded in the relevant authority list and
                    points from there to its class in this file.</p>
                <p>Naming conventions. Identifiers are lowercase and use the hyphen, never the
                    underscore. Where a category names an act, the identifier is the lemma of the
                    verb in the first person singular present indicative, the citation form of
                    Latin lexicography and of the Latin computational resources, so that these
                    categories join the <att>lemma</att> of the verbs in the editions without an
                    intervening mapping. Two categories are in English rather than Latin, each for
                    a stated reason given in its own <gi>gloss</gi>: Latin would have required
                    either coining a term, which this project does not do, or reusing one that is
                    already taken by another vocabulary of this same corpus.</p>
            </projectDesc>
